From: Kervin Pursoty <kpursoty@proton.me>
Subject: mips: early_printk_8250: poll THRE only, not TEMT

prom_putchar() in early_printk_8250.c currently polls both THRE (bit 5,
TX holding register empty) and TEMT (bit 6, TX shift register empty)
before writing a character. On the Airoha EN7523 UART (used in EcoNet
EN751627 / ZyXEL EX3301-T0 and related big-endian SoCs), TEMT is not
reliably asserted between characters.

With a finite timeout, prom_putchar() times out on the TEMT check and
silently drops the character (the write only happens inside the
"if (timeout)" branch). The result is that all early kernel output
is lost and the kernel appears to hang.

The stock EcoNet/Airoha kernel polls only THRE, which is the correct
condition: THRE means the TX holding register is ready to accept the
next byte. Polling TEMT (shift register also drained) is unnecessarily
strict and causes problems on platforms that don't assert it reliably.

Change prom_putchar() to poll THRE only. This is safe for all MIPS
platforms: a UART ready for the next byte always asserts THRE.

Signed-off-by: Kervin Pursoty <kpursoty@proton.me>
---
--- a/arch/mips/kernel/early_printk_8250.c
+++ b/arch/mips/kernel/early_printk_8250.c
@@ -42,5 +42,10 @@ void prom_putchar(char c)
 	timeout = serial8250_tx_timeout;
-	bits = UART_LSR_TEMT | UART_LSR_THRE;
+	/*
+	 * Poll THRE (TX holding register empty) only. TEMT (shift register
+	 * empty, bit 6) is not reliable on all platforms (e.g. Airoha EN7523)
+	 * and polling it causes prom_putchar to drop characters on timeout.
+	 */
+	bits = UART_LSR_THRE;
 
 	do {
 		status = serial_in(UART_LSR);
